    {'subject':'Chemistry','topic':'Electrolysis','topic_code':'C4','difficulty':2,
     'question_text':'During electrolysis of copper sulfate solution with inert electrodes, what forms at the cathode?',
     'option_a':'Oxygen','option_b':'Hydrogen','option_c':'Copper','option_d':'Sulfur',
     'correct_answer':'C','explanation':'Cu²⁺ + 2e⁻ → Cu. Copper ions are preferentially discharged at the cathode because copper is below hydrogen in the reactivity series.','correction_tip':'Cu is below H in reactivity, so Cu²⁺ is discharged before H⁺ at cathode.'},

